summary:
- topic: FY26 Budget Presentation
  text: The superintendent presented the first FY26 budget workshop, outlining a proposed
    budget with a nearly 6% increase, requiring an additional $3.17 million from taxpayers.
    The presentation framed the budget within the context of significant financial
    challenges.
- topic: Enrollment and Staffing Trends
  text: Key data showed a projected 10% student enrollment decline since 2022, reaching
    a 15-year low. In contrast, staffing levels have increased over a similar period,
    creating a structural imbalance the budget aims to address.
- topic: Property Tax Impact
  text: A major focus was the heavy tax burden on residents. Due to a city-wide property
    revaluation combined with a mill rate increase, the average single-family home's
    school tax bill is projected to rise by 38% (over $1,100) year-over-year.
- topic: Proposed Staff and Program Reductions
  text: To mitigate the tax increase, the budget proposes numerous reductions, including
    the Director of Curriculum, three Social Emotional Learning (SEL) teachers, two
    ESOL teachers, a full-time percussion ed tech, and the elimination of the second
    lunch program.
- topic: Public Comment
  text: An extensive public comment period featured passionate opposition from teachers,
    parents, and students. Speakers argued the cuts would harm student mental health,
    academic quality, and vital programs in music and ESOL, and questioned the transparency
    of the budget process.
- topic: Board Discussion
  text: Board members questioned the rationale for specific cuts, particularly the
    Director of Curriculum, and debated the difficult balance between fiscal responsibility
    to taxpayers and maintaining high-quality educational programs and student supports.
- topic: Athletic Complex and Future Funding
  text: The superintendent clarified that the proposed SPHS Athletic Complex bond
    is a separate voter decision in November and has no financial impact on the FY26
    operating budget. The administration also announced plans to establish an Education
    Foundation for future alternative funding.
